    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm reviewing a commercial retail lease in Singapore and need a quick breakdown of how a standard force majeure clause usually operates when there's a prolonged business interruption. Specifically, I need to know if the tenant typically has grounds to suspend rent obligations under such a clause if the premises become unusable due to external circumstances beyond their control.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under standard commercial retail lease conventions in Singapore, a force majeure clause typically operates as a shield against liability rather than a sword to suspend rent obligations. While the clause may excuse the tenant from performing specific covenants (like paying rent) if the premises become unusable due to an event like a natural disaster, the \"prolonged business interruption\" aspect is often handled differently. Most leases require the tenant to mitigate losses and may still expect rent to be paid unless the lease explicitly states that rent becomes due only upon the tenant's actual occupation of the premises.\n\nHowever, the devil is always in the details, and you must check if the specific wording includes a \"rent abatement\" or \"rent suspension\" mechanism triggered by the event. If the clause is silent on rent, the default position is often that rent remains payable, and the tenant might only be entitled to a refund of the portion of rent corresponding to the period they were unable to use the premises, rather than a total suspension. It is crucial to read the \"consequences\" subsection of the clause carefully, as some include a \"rent holiday\" while others merely allow for termination if the interruption exceeds a certain threshold (often 12 months).\n\nIn the spirit of this",
      "source": "target",
