    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you go more into the specifics of how \"unforeseeable circumstances\" are defined in these clauses? I'm particularly interested in cases where the interruption isn't a natural disaster, but something like a government-mandated lockdown or a sudden regulatory change. How do these typically fit under force majeure in a Singaporean context?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Definitely, let's dig into the \"unforeseeable circumstances\" definition, which is often the most contentious part of any lease, especially when it comes to things like government-mandated lockdowns or sudden regulatory changes. In Singapore, these are frequently treated under the umbrella of \"Acts of God\" or \"Acts of Public Authority,\" but the definition can vary wildly depending on whether the clause explicitly lists \"pandemics,\" \"quarantines,\" or \"government orders\" as specific triggers.\n\nIf the clause is vague and just says \"unforeseeable,\" a court or arbitrator might look at the *reasonableness* of the event at the time the lease was signed. For example, during the pandemic, many leases were amended to explicitly include \"pandemic-related restrictions\" because the original wording was too broad to cover a global health crisis that no one could have predicted in 2019. Without that specific amendment, a tenant might argue that a lockdown was foreseeable given the global context, whereas a sudden, unexpected change in zoning laws or a new environmental regulation might be deemed truly unforeseeable.\n\nHowever, even if the event is \"unforeseeable,\" the definition often includes a \"",
      "source": "target",
